  "multiplexed_traffic_experiment": {
    "assessment_topic": "Outer-ESP Encapsulated Background Traffic Multiplexing",
    "feasibility": "ARCHITECTURALLY_BLOCKED_FOR_SINGLE_ESP_SA",
    "explanation": "Under the vista-flow-v1 contract, the outer flow key is defined by network-layer protocol (50) and canonical outer IP:port endpoints (172.20.0.2:0 <-> 172.20.0.3:0). Because inner IP and transport headers (TCP ports, ICMP headers, sequence numbers) are encrypted within ESP, all traffic traversing the PC1-PC2 IPsec tunnel shares the exact same outer flow identifier. If background traffic traverses the tunnel simultaneously with a target workload, both flows are merged into a single aggregate outer flow in the packet table. The resulting flow features (packet_count, byte_count, std_packet_size, mean_inter_arrival_time) aggregate both streams, violating the 1-flow-per-experiment ground-truth contract. Disentangling multiplexed application streams without inner plaintext inspection or separate negotiated CHILD_SAs with dedicated SPIs is mathematically impossible.",
    "recommendation": "Future work may investigate tunnel-level aggregate traffic characterization or separate CHILD_SAs per workload type, but isolated-flow ground truth cannot be assigned to mixed aggregate ESP frames."
  },
